Evaluation of an approximable map at an argument, as a binary approximable map on the function space.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`eval`).** Evaluation relates a function-space neighbourhood, a domain neighbourhood, and a codomain neighbourhood precisely when every map in the former relates the latter two.

*Proof.* Domain and codomain membership unpack the conjuncts; the masters relate by the masters of each system and the master law for each map; intersection on the right and monotonicity use the corresponding laws for each map in the function-space neighbourhood. ∎

**Definition (`evalMap`).** Evaluation as a joint approximable map out of the product of a function space with its domain is the product form of binary evaluation.

*Proof.* Apply the product encoding of binary approximable maps. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementMap₂_eval`).** Binary evaluation of an ideal element of a function space at an ideal element of the domain recovers application of the corresponding approximable map.

*Proof.* Compare membership: upward closure with a step neighbourhood yields one inclusion; the converse witnesses evaluation at that step. ∎

**Theorem (`evalMap_apply`).** Joint evaluation at a paired ideal element equals application of the recovered approximable map.

*Proof.* Unpack the product encoding and apply the binary evaluation identity. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementMap_evalMap_comp_paired`).** Evaluation after pairing a map into a function space with a map into the domain acts elementwise by applying the recovered value of the first to the value of the second.

*Proof.* Compose the elementwise pairing and evaluation identities. ∎
